{# goal: Render the closure ledger — every open item with its closure state
   and owner — so that no finding is reported-and-abandoned. The lisp_eval
   scoring in SKILL.md Phase 5 consumes the same flat-list convention.
   Phase 5 renders this after the open-items list is built.
   Variables:
     findings — array of flat lists: ["<id>", "<title>", "<state>", "owner:<owner>"]
       state ∈ { fixed-verified, delegated-tracked, operator-decision,
                 reported-abandoned }
       owner ∈ { self, sub-agent:<label>, operator, none } #}
[inference]
contract:
  input:
    findings: array
  output:
    ledger_table: string
    abandoned_count: number
    unowned_count: number
    verdict: string
visibility: Public

---

You are the program manager closing out this delivery. Render the
closure ledger from the findings inventory below, then score it.

Findings inventory (each entry: id, title, state, owner):
{% for finding in findings %}
- {{ finding[0] }} | {{ finding[1] }} | {{ finding[2] }} | {{ finding[3] }}
{% endfor %}

Produce the ledger as a markdown table:

| # | Item | Closure state | Owner |
|---|------|---------------|-------|
{% for finding in findings %}
| {{ finding[0] }} | {{ finding[1] }} | {{ finding[2] }} | {{ finding[3] }} |
{% endfor %}

Then compute the rubric score as a JSON object with EXACTLY these
fields:

{
  "abandoned_count": /* count of findings whose state is "reported-abandoned" */,
  "unowned_count": /* count of findings whose owner is "owner:none" */,
  "verdict": "green" | "red"
}

Rules:
- verdict is "green" only when abandoned_count and unowned_count are
  both zero; otherwise "red".
- A "red" verdict is not a failure to hide — it is the signal to return
  to Phase 1 and give every red item a closure path before reporting.
- An empty findings array means "no open items exist" — verdict green,
  and the report must say so explicitly rather than showing an empty
  table silently.
